ons for navigating the system.\n*   There are no unusual visual conditions or changes visible in the image.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n*   **Observed Facts**\n    *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n    *   The category is Password Reset - Escalated.\n    *   The requester is J. Employee.\n    *   The employee ID is SOL-04821.\n    *   The manager is A. Menon.\n    *   The department is Finance.\n    *   The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n*   **Inference**\n    *   The ticket is likely related to a password reset issue, given the category.\n    *   The requester and employee ID suggest that the issue may be specific to a particular employee or department.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, but the text is not particularly large or prominent, which may make it slightly difficult to read. Additionally, the context of the ticketing system is not explicitly stated, which may limit the ability to infer additional information.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.50 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a navigation menu on the left and a central section displaying user information. The user's name is SOL-04821, and they are identified as an employee in the Finance department.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The navigation menu on the left includes options for Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n    *   The central section displays the user's information, including their name, ID, and department.\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user's name is SOL-04821.\n    *   They are identified as an employee in the Finance department.\n*   **Navigation Menu**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the screen.\n    *   It includes options for Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a navigation menu on the left and a central section displaying user information. The user's name is SOL-04821, and they are identified as an employee in the Finance department.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The navigation menu on the left includes options for Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n    *   The central section displays the user's information, including their name, ID, and department.\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user's name is SOL-04821.\n    *   They are identified as an employee in the Finance department.\n*   **Navigation Menu**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the screen.\n    *   It includes options for Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The user's name is SOL-04821.\n    *   They are identified as an employee in the Finance department.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The user may be experiencing issues with their account or permissions.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.5\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the user's issue is not explicitly stated.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center containing a list of options for identity verification. The title of the box is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n\n*   The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n*   The white box in the center contains a list of options for identity verification.\n    *   The options include:\n        *   Employee ID matches ticket\n        *   Manager name matches ticket\n        *   Department matches record\n        *   Last 4 digits of phone on file\n*   The background of the image is dark gray.\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a list of options for identity verification. The options include Employee ID matches ticket, Manager name matches ticket, Department matches record, and Last 4 digits of phone on file.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n*   The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n*   The white box in the center contains a list of options for identity verification.\n*   The options include:\n    *   Employee ID matches ticket\n    *   Manager name matches ticket\n    *   Department matches record\n    *   Last 4 digits of phone on file\n*   The background of the image is dark gray.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   OBSERVED FACTS:\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n    *   The white box in the center contains a list of options for identity verification.\n    *   The options include Employee ID matches ticket, Manager name matches ticket, Department matches record, and Last 4 digits of phone on file.\n*   INFERENCE:\n    *   The user is attempting to verify an employee's identity.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and the text is legible.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a login page for an application called \"CoreID\". The page has a white background with black text and a blue button.\n\n*   **Application Name:** CoreID\n*   **Login Page:** The page is a login page with a white background and black text.\n*   **Button:** There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\" in white text.\n*   **Error Message:** There is an error message that says \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" in black text.\n*   **Temporary Password:** The temporary password is displayed in black text and is \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n*   **Force Change at Next Sign-in:** There is a checkbox that says \"Force change at next sign-in\" in black text.\n*   **Button:** There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\" in white text.\n\n**EXTRACTED TEXT:**\n\n*   \"Reset Password - J. Employee\"\n*   \"Temporary password Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\"\n*   \"Force change at next sign-in\"\n\n**ERRORS AND WARNINGS:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY:**\n\n*   Category: Software/application\n*   Severity: Medium\n*   Reason: The error message suggests that the user's password needs to be reset, but the severity is medium because it is not a critical issue.\n\n**OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE:**\n\n*   **OBSERVED FACTS:**\n    *   The application is called CoreID.\n    *   The page is a login page.\n    *   There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n    *   There is an error message that says \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n    *   The temporary password is \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n    *   There is a checkbox that says \"Force change at next sign-in\".\n*   **INFERENCE:**\n    *   The user's password needs to be reset.\n    *   The user may have forgotten their password or it may have been compromised.\n\n**CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and the text is legible, making it easy to understand the issue.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   Dashboard\n    *   Tickets\n    *   Users\n    *   Reports\n    *   Settings\n\nThe image suggests that the ticket has been resolved, and the resolution notes indicate that the identity has been verified, a temporary password has been issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature has been enabled. There are no error messages or warnings visible in the image.\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   **Category:** Software/Application\n*   **Severity:** Medium\n\nThe issue appears to be related to the CoreID Admin Console, specifically with the ticket resolution process. The severity is medium because the issue has been resolved, but there may still be some underlying problems that need to be addressed.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The ticket has been resolved.\n    *   The identity has been verified.\n    *   A temporary password has been issued.\n    *   The force-change-at-next-sign-in feature has been enabled.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The issue may have been caused by a security breach or unauthorized access.\n    *   The temporary password may have been issued to prevent further unauthorized access.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   **Confidence Score:** 0.95\n*   **Confidence Level:** High\n*   **Confidence Reason:** The image is clear, and the text is legible. The resolution notes provide specific details about the issue and the actions taken to resolve it.\n\nOverall, the image suggests that the ticket has been resolved, and the issue has been addressed. However, there may still be some underlying problems that need to be addressed. The confidence score is high because the image is clear, and the text is legible.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secu